WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.203 --> 00:00:13.524
policeman on the beat moved up the avenue impressively.

2
00:00:14.424 --> 00:00:18.865
The impressiveness was habitual and not for show, for spectators were few.

3
00:00:19.826 --> 00:00:25.207
The time was barely ten o'clock at night, but chilly gusts of wind with a taste of rain

4
00:00:25.247 --> 00:00:28.088
in them had well-nigh depeopled the streets.

5
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:36.185
Trying doors as he went, twirling his club with many intricate and artful movements, turning now and then

6
00:00:36.265 --> 00:00:43.591
to cast his watchful eye down the pacific thoroughfare, the officer, with his stalwart form and slight swagger,

7
00:00:44.011 --> 00:00:46.373
made a fine picture of a guardian of the peace.

8
00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:50.236
The vicinity was one that kept early hours.

9
00:00:51.016 --> 00:00:55.420
Now and then, you might see the lights of a cigar store or of an all-night lunch counter;

10
00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.723
but the majority of the doors belonged to business places that had long since been closed.

11
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:06.526
When about midway of a certain block, the policeman suddenly slowed his walk.

12
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:14.090
In the doorway of a darkened hardware store, a man leaned, with an unlighted cigar in his mouth.

13
00:01:15.091 --> 00:01:18.393
As the policeman walked up to him, the man spoke up quickly.

14
00:01:18.413 --> 00:01:21.735
"It's all right, officer," he said reassuringly.

15
00:01:21.755 --> 00:01:23.476
"I'm just waiting for a friend.

16
00:01:24.212 --> 00:01:25.913
It's an appointment made twenty years ago.

17
00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:28.274
Sounds a little funny to you, doesn't it?

18
00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.836
Well, I'll explain if you'd like to make certain it's all straight.

19
00:01:32.556 --> 00:01:38.239
About that long ago, there used to be a restaurant where this store stands—‘Big Joe' Brady's restaurant."

20
00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:41.641
"Until five years ago," said the policeman.

21
00:01:41.661 --> 00:01:43.342
"It was torn down then."

22
00:01:44.402 --> 00:01:47.404
The man in the doorway struck a match and lit his cigar.

23
00:01:48.288 --> 00:01:54.170
The light showed a pale, square-jawed face with keen eyes and a little white scar near his right

24
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:58.971
eyebrow. His scarfpin was a large diamond, oddly set.

25
00:02:00.272 --> 00:02:05.733
"Twenty years ago tonight," said the man, "I dined here at 'Big Joe' Brady's with Jimmy Wells, my

26
00:02:05.813 --> 00:02:08.114
best chum and the finest chap in the world.

27
00:02:08.874 --> 00:02:12.315
He and I were raised here in New York, just like two brothers, together.

28
00:02:13.275 --> 00:02:16.024
I was eighteen and Jimmy was twenty.

29
00:02:16.044 --> 00:02:19.325
The next morning, I was to start for the West to make my fortune.

30
00:02:20.465 --> 00:02:24.526
You couldn't have dragged Jimmy out of New York; he thought it was the only place on earth.

31
00:02:25.527 --> 00:02:30.908
Well, we agreed that night that we would meet here again exactly twenty years from that date and

32
00:02:30.968 --> 00:02:36.210
time, no matter what our conditions might be or from what distance we might have to come.

33
00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:41.451
We figured that in twenty years each of us ought to have our destiny worked out and our

34
00:02:41.511 --> 00:02:43.912
fortunes made, whatever they were going to be."

35
00:02:44.688 --> 00:02:47.310
"It sounds pretty interesting," said the policeman.

36
00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:51.052
"Rather a long time between meets, though, it seems to me.

37
00:02:51.872 --> 00:02:53.793
Haven't you heard from your friend since you left?"

38
00:02:53.813 --> 00:02:58.036
"Well, yes, for a time we corresponded," said the other.

39
00:02:58.056 --> 00:03:01.718
"But after a year or two, we lost track of each other.

40
00:03:02.478 --> 00:03:09.042
You see, the West is a pretty big proposition, and I kept hustling around over it pretty lively.

41
00:03:09.882 --> 00:03:15.870
But I know Jimmy will meet me here if he's alive, for he always was the truest, staunchest

42
00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:16.910
old chap in the world.

43
00:03:17.791 --> 00:03:18.691
He'll never forget.

44
00:03:19.372 --> 00:03:23.614
I came a thousand miles to stand in this door tonight, and it's worth it if my old

45
00:03:23.694 --> 00:03:24.594
partner turns up."

46
00:03:25.595 --> 00:03:30.558
The waiting man pulled out a handsome watch, the lids of it set with small diamonds.

47
00:03:30.578 --> 00:03:33.399
"Three minutes to ten," he announced.

48
00:03:33.419 --> 00:03:37.441
"It was exactly ten o'clock when we parted here at the restaurant door."

49
00:03:37.461 --> 00:03:40.423
"Did pretty well out West, didn't you?"

50
00:03:41.043 --> 00:03:42.004
asked the policeman.

51
00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:43.489
"You bet!

52
00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:45.831
I hope Jimmy has done half as well.

53
00:03:46.611 --> 00:03:49.533
He was a kind of plodder, though, good fellow as he was.

54
00:03:50.935 --> 00:03:54.558
I've had to compete with some of the sharpest wits going to get my pile.

55
00:03:55.338 --> 00:03:57.100
A man gets in a groove in New York.

56
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.202
It takes the West to put a razor edge on him."

57
00:04:00.923 --> 00:04:03.745
The policeman twirled his club and took a step or two.

58
00:04:03.765 --> 00:04:05.546
"I'll be on my way.

59
00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:07.708
Hope your friend comes around all right.

60
00:04:08.228 --> 00:04:09.750
Going to call time on him sharp?"

61
00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:11.631
"I should say not!"

62
00:04:11.911 --> 00:04:12.432
said the other.

63
00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.801
"I'll give him half an hour at least.

64
00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:18.603
If Jimmy is alive on earth, he'll be here by that time.

65
00:04:19.283 --> 00:04:20.264
So long, officer."

66
00:04:20.284 --> 00:04:26.987
"Good night, sir," said the policeman, passing on along his beat, trying doors as he went.

67
00:04:27.988 --> 00:04:34.551
There was now a fine, cold drizzle falling, and the wind had risen from its uncertain puffs into

68
00:04:34.591 --> 00:04:35.412
a steady blow.

69
00:04:36.288 --> 00:04:43.391
The few foot passengers in that quarter hurried dismally and silently along with coat collars turned high and

70
00:04:43.491 --> 00:04:44.471
pocketed hands.

71
00:04:45.812 --> 00:04:50.354
And in the door of the hardware store, the man who had come a thousand miles to fill

72
00:04:50.374 --> 00:04:57.157
an appointment, uncertain almost to absurdity, with the friend of his youth, smoked his cigar and waited.

73
00:04:58.257 --> 00:05:04.300
About twenty minutes he waited, and then a tall man in a long overcoat, with collar turned up

74
00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:07.737
to his ears, hurried across from the opposite side of the street.

75
00:05:08.717 --> 00:05:10.437
He went directly to the waiting man.

76
00:05:10.457 --> 00:05:12.238
"Is that you, Bob?"

77
00:05:12.878 --> 00:05:13.858
he asked doubtfully.

78
00:05:13.878 --> 00:05:16.179
"Is that you, Jimmy Wells?"

79
00:05:16.559 --> 00:05:17.559
cried the man in the door.

80
00:05:17.579 --> 00:05:19.560
"Bless my heart!"

81
00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:23.561
exclaimed the new arrival, grasping both the other's hands with his own.

82
00:05:23.581 --> 00:05:26.042
"It's Bob, sure as fate.

83
00:05:26.842 --> 00:05:29.683
I was certain I'd find you here if you were still in existence.

84
00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:31.703
Well, well, well!

85
00:05:32.003 --> 00:05:33.504
Twenty years is a long time.

86
00:05:34.304 --> 00:05:39.267
The old restaurant's gone, Bob; I wish it had lasted, so we could have had another dinner there.

87
00:05:40.088 --> 00:05:41.909
How has the West treated you, old man?"

88
00:05:41.929 --> 00:05:46.031
"Bully; it has given me everything I asked it for.

89
00:05:46.591 --> 00:05:47.912
You've changed lots, Jimmy.

90
00:05:48.412 --> 00:05:51.054
I never thought you were so tall by two or three inches."

91
00:05:51.074 --> 00:05:53.796
"Oh, I grew a bit after I was twenty."

92
00:05:53.816 --> 00:05:55.677
"Doing well in New York, Jimmy?"

93
00:05:55.697 --> 00:05:59.559
"Moderately. I have a position in one of the city departments.

94
00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:04.459
Come on, Bob; we'll go around to a place I know of, and have a good long talk

95
00:06:04.499 --> 00:06:05.440
about old times."

96
00:06:06.321 --> 00:06:09.163
The two men started up the street, arm in arm.

97
00:06:09.784 --> 00:06:15.709
The man from the West, his egotism enlarged by success, was beginning to outline the history of his

98
00:06:15.769 --> 00:06:21.134
career. The other, submerged in his overcoat, listened with interest.

99
00:06:22.154 --> 00:06:25.798
At the corner stood a drug store, brilliant with electric lights.

100
00:06:26.678 --> 00:06:32.155
When they came into this glare, each of them turned simultaneously to gaze upon the other's face.

101
00:06:33.076 --> 00:06:36.199
The man from the West stopped suddenly and released his arm.

102
00:06:36.219 --> 00:06:39.882
"You're not Jimmy Wells," he snapped.

103
00:06:40.423 --> 00:06:44.706
"Twenty years is a long time, but not long enough to change a man's nose from a Roman

104
00:06:44.766 --> 00:06:45.447
to a pug."

105
00:06:45.467 --> 00:06:50.812
"It sometimes changes a good man into a bad one," said the tall man.

106
00:06:50.832 --> 00:06:55.456
"You've been under arrest for ten minutes, 'Silky' Bob.

107
00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.043
Chicago thinks you may have dropped over our way and wires us she wants to have a chat

108
00:07:00.063 --> 00:07:00.383
with you.

109
00:07:01.644 --> 00:07:03.065
Going quietly, are you?

110
00:07:03.445 --> 00:07:04.346
That's sensible.

111
00:07:05.487 --> 00:07:09.910
Now, before we go on to the station, here's a note I was asked to hand you.

112
00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:11.911
You may read it here at the window.

113
00:07:12.612 --> 00:07:14.213
It's from Patrolman Wells."

114
00:07:15.293 --> 00:07:18.956
The man from the West unfolded the little piece of paper handed him.

115
00:07:19.776 --> 00:07:24.119
His hand was steady when he began to read, but it trembled a little by the time he

116
00:07:24.139 --> 00:07:24.700
had finished.

117
00:07:25.216 --> 00:07:26.658
The note was rather short.

118
00:07:27.679 --> 00:07:30.341
Bob: I was at the appointed place on time.

119
00:07:31.202 --> 00:07:35.426
When you struck the match to light your cigar, I saw it was the face of the man

120
00:07:35.486 --> 00:07:36.547
wanted in Chicago.

121
00:07:37.448 --> 00:07:42.834
Somehow, I couldn't do it myself, so I went around and got a plainclothes man to do the

122
00:07:42.874 --> 00:07:44.055
job. Jimmy
